WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.140
Despite the satisfying way "F9" continues
the stories of Dom, Letty, and all the rest,

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.500
there are a number of questions we've been
left to ponder since the credits rolled.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.580
Here are some of the biggest.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.000
The newest — and wildest — adventure in
the "Fast and Furious" saga is kicked off

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.300
when Mr. Nobody sends an encrypted distress
signal meant only for Vin Diesel’s character,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.439
Dom Toretto, and his team.

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:23.710
In his message, he says his plane is going
down with dangerous cargo on board.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:28.109
Ramsey is the one who picks it up, and wastes
no time in getting most of the team back together.

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:32.390
After some light persuasion, she and Dom,
along with Letty, Roman, and Tej are on board.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.520
At the crash site, however, there's no trace
of Mr. Nobody.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.129
After doing a cursory sweep for him and coming
up empty, Dom's crew never seems to spend

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:41.690
any more energy trying to figure out if he's
still alive.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.980
Sure, it looked like a pretty devastating
crash, but considering Mr. Nobody’s apparent

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.870
ability to arrange the impossible, it seems
as though nothing is beyond his grasp.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:55.190
Speaking of Mr. Nobody and the impossible,
Han is apparently alive in "F9," despite the

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:58.559
seemingly deadly crash and murder attempt
in "Tokyo Drift."

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:02.989
The same scene which we saw revisited again
in "Fast &amp; Furious 6" and "Furious 7" — except

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:05.820
the explanation doesn't actually account for
what happened.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.240
According to Han, he was recruited as an operative
by Mr. Nobody.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.930
Apparently, this happened sometime between
the end of "Fast &amp; Furious 6" and the beginning

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:17.900
of "Tokyo Drift" which chronologically takes
place between the sixth and seventh films.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.330
Sometime before Sean Boswell showed up in
Tokyo, Han did a job for Mr. Nobody that required

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:23.500
him to stay hidden.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.400
Mr. Nobody, then, determined the best way
to do this was to fake Han's death, so no

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.920
one would be looking for him.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.910
It's a neat workaround for bringing back the
character, except for one thing: the car chase

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.431
Han was involved in when he "died" was completely
impromptu.

00:01:35.431 --> 00:01:40.380
In "Tokyo Drift," we see that Han is fleeing
his former business partner after he got caught

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.380
stealing.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.540
Previously, it seemed as though Deckard Shaw
took advantage of that opportunity to take

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.170
Han out by T-boning him with his own car.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.170
But now we are to understand that Mr. Nobody
somehow staged the entire thing.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:55.700
Since we definitely saw that Han was physically
present for the entire chase in "Tokyo Drift,"

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.280
even seeing the crash from inside his car,
there's no way he could have somehow escaped

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.450
the car before Deckard crashed into him.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:05.870
Was Mr. Nobody lurking around, ready to extract
him the instant an opportunity presented itself?

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:09.570
If so, how did he get Han out of the wrecked
car without anyone noticing?

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:14.320
"F9" gives us some answers, but really only
raises more questions.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.640
While Mr. Nobody's presence looms large over
the plot of "F9," even if he isn't physically

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.880
around, his protege is nowhere to be found,
and isn't even mentioned.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.330
Introduced in "Fate of the Furious," Little
Nobody, who’s played by Scott Eastwood,

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:29.410
worked alongside the rest of the crew in thwarting
their enemy, Cipher.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.500
Little Nobody was at the family barbecue at
the end of the film, which is typically a

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.140
pretty solid indicator that a character has
ascended to the rank of "family" and will

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.770
continue to pop up in future installments.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.090
Even still, he's completely absent in "F9."

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:45.480
Now, it’s not a stretch to say that Little
Nobody didn't make much of an impression in

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.480
"Fate of the Furious."

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.900
Unlike most of the other recurring "Fast and
Furious" characters, who each have personality

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.200
by the bucketful, he was by nature pretty
bland and didn't add much to the crew.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.730
Granted, this was partially because he was
a covert government agent who was supposed

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:02.040
to be able to disappear, but even Mr. Nobody
managed to live under the radar while still

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.730
being an engaging character.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:07.209
So perhaps he was left out simply because
he wouldn't be particularly missed.

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:11.650
Still, it's a little odd that with a plot
that so heavily ties into Mr. Nobody's plans,

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.160
no one would even think to mention looking
for his second in command.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.209
"F9" actually does a good job of explaining
why Dom and Mia have never mentioned they

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:23.230
had another brother before, and have erased
him from all their childhood stories.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.760
It turns out that in the race that killed
Dom's father, which he told Brian about in

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.420
the first movie, his car had been tampered
with, causing it to respond poorly during

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:31.420
the race.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:35.099
Teenage Dom realized it was his younger brother
Jakob who had messed with the car, and assumed

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:37.410
he'd sabotaged their father on purpose.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.290
Dom banished Jakob from the family, and never
spoke of him again.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.520
However, it’s later revealed that Jakob
stayed with a friend of their dad’s named

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.410
Buddy nearby for about a year before moving
on.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.680
This is a little odd, seeing that Dom or Mia
would have possibly crossed paths with Jakob

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.330
again after he left the Toretto home.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.200
After all, Buddy seemed pretty close to the
Toretto family.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.790
Did Jakob have to leave the house and pretend
that he wasn't living there if Dom or Mia

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.040
pass by Buddy's house?

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.670
That seems to be the only way they could've
avoided running into one another and triggering

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.970
an extremely awkward conversation.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.700
Dom says in the film that he knew Jakob was
living with Buddy, so maybe the elder Toretto

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.520
just avoided Buddy for a while.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.440
Dom soon realizes that the person responsible
for stealing the device capable of hijacking

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.680
every superweapon in the world is his estranged
brother Jakob, played by John Cena.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.629
So, Dom sets off to track down his long-lost
sibling.

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:26.580
First, he visits Buddy and learns Jakob has
gone to London.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.409
Then, he heads to London, where he asks Magdalene
Shaw what she can find out through her underworld

00:04:30.409 --> 00:04:31.409
connections.

00:04:31.409 --> 00:04:35.270
Sure enough, she knows right where he can
find Jakob, and drives him right to his brother's

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:36.270
doorstep.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.889
But while Dom's detective work is impressive,
it also raises some questions.

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:42.400
First of all, how did he even know where to
find Magdalene Shaw?

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.939
More importantly, why did he have to do all
this tracking in the first place when he could've

00:04:45.939 --> 00:04:47.610
just used the God's Eye?

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.229
First introduced in "Furious 7," the God's
Eye is a piece of tracking software that can

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:54.020
be used to find anyone on the planet in seconds.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:57.139
The crew used it again in "Fate of the Furious"
to track down Cipher.

00:04:57.139 --> 00:05:00.759
Plus, Dom has the inventor of the God's Eye,
Ramsey, on his crew.

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:03.870
So why the pond-hopping when Dom could've
cut straight to the chase?

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.479
Did they simply forget they had the world's
most advanced tracking technology at their

00:05:07.479 --> 00:05:08.979
disposal?

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:12.999
So let's address the elephant in the room
right away: there are a lot of issues with

00:05:12.999 --> 00:05:14.919
Roman and Tej’s space plan.

00:05:14.919 --> 00:05:20.430
“Please tell me that’s not a Pontiac Fiero
strapped to a rocket engine.”

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.069
Most of these issues we're willing to let
slide because this is a "Fast and Furious"

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:27.430
movie and realism has never been a major selling
point for this franchise.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:31.520
However, it seems a little odd that after
defying all odds and managing to destroy the

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.229
satellite they were sent to disarm, they are
then able to essentially hitchhike home with

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:39.240
the astronauts aboard the International Space
Station without any negative consequences.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.610
Granted, we don't actually have any clue what
would happen if two men in scuba suits wound

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:47.379
up floating by in a Pontiac Fiero outside
the International Space Station.

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:50.180
Hardly seems like the sort of thing anyone
would plan for.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.430
But it does seem like it should be more severe
than just eating Tootsie Rolls for a couple

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:57.169
weeks and then being allowed to tag along
on their trip back to Earth.

00:05:57.169 --> 00:06:01.550
While it's possible Roman and Tej escaped
on a technicality, considering the possibility

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:05.849
that there aren't actually any laws governing
what they did, the same can't be said for

00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:07.099
everyone else.

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:10.659
Dom and his crew caused a massive destruction
while working to stop Otto from gaining the

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:11.849
power to destroy the world.

00:06:11.849 --> 00:06:16.069
This isn't the first time Dom's crew has leveled
several city blocks or destroyed dozens of

00:06:16.069 --> 00:06:17.069
cars.

00:06:17.069 --> 00:06:20.779
However, in previous films, they either had
to go on the run afterward or were working

00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:24.059
for the government, which presumably helped
clean up the mess.

00:06:24.059 --> 00:06:26.199
This time, they're effectively on their own.

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:30.039
This raises the question of why they're able
to relax at a family barbecue at the end instead

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:33.999
of being arrested for causing tens of millions
of dollars worth of property damage.

00:06:33.999 --> 00:06:37.669
Not even including all the cars they destroy,
they also take out multiple buildings with

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:39.529
their magnetic shenanigans.

00:06:39.529 --> 00:06:43.440
It seems a little weird that no one needs
to pay — literally — for all the destruction

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.990
they caused, even if it happened in the name
of saving the world.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:50.279
“No one needs to know about this.”

00:06:50.279 --> 00:06:54.009
Once again, just like at the end of "Fate
of the Furious," Cipher manages to evade capture

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:57.669
at the end of "F9," even though the rest of
the team she's working with isn't nearly so

00:06:57.669 --> 00:06:58.669
lucky.

00:06:58.669 --> 00:07:01.509
Of course, sometime between the ending of
"Fate of the Furious" and the beginning of

00:07:01.509 --> 00:07:05.319
"F9," Mr. Nobody somehow managed to track
her down and capture her, so we know that

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:06.349
such a thing is possible.

00:07:06.349 --> 00:07:10.110
But at the same time, he didn't manage to
hold on to her for long.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.379
Plus, we have no idea where Mr. Nobody even
is anymore, or how to get a hold of his covert

00:07:14.379 --> 00:07:15.629
organization.

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:19.110
And we definitely expect Cipher to be even
more careful this time about covering her

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:20.210
tracks.

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.930
So it seems like the odds of her being found
before she wants to are low, and that whenever

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:28.039
she does reappear, it will be in a fittingly
dramatic and shocking fashion.

00:07:28.039 --> 00:07:32.729
"F9" made an impressive effort to bring back
all sorts of familiar faces from previous

00:07:32.729 --> 00:07:33.759
films.

00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:37.619
Even Brian was implied to be present at the
final barbecue, although we only got to see

00:07:37.619 --> 00:07:38.629
his car.

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:43.339
But one former member of the team was noticeably
absent: Luke Hobbs, played by Dwayne Johnson,

00:07:43.339 --> 00:07:46.159
who's been an integral member of the crew
since "Fast Five."

00:07:46.159 --> 00:07:50.059
Behind the scenes, Johnson is busy with his
own spinoff franchise, "Hobbs and Shaw," as

00:07:50.059 --> 00:07:52.240
well as numerous other projects.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.379
But just because the actor wasn't available
doesn't mean we aren't wondering where the

00:07:55.379 --> 00:07:56.819
character is.

00:07:56.819 --> 00:08:00.129
It seems that given the high stakes and difficult
nature of their mission, it would've made

00:08:00.129 --> 00:08:01.960
sense to call in the big guns.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:05.849
But Hobbs isn't even mentioned in "F9," making
us wonder why the characters wouldn't have

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:07.270
thought to call him.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:10.929
Perhaps they already knew he wouldn't be available;
maybe he's spending more time with his daughter,

00:08:10.929 --> 00:08:15.669
or visiting his family in Samoa, whom he reconnected
with in "Hobbs and Shaw.”

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:17.970
“Daddy’s staying home.”

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.029
There are any number of places he could have
been, but it feels a little weird that the

00:08:25.029 --> 00:08:27.229
movie doesn't even hint at where he was.

00:08:27.229 --> 00:08:32.570
Early in "F9," after surviving several situations
that should've reasonably resulted in his

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:36.630
death, Roman postulates that perhaps the reason
he keeps defying death is because he and the

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.310
rest of the crew are actually invincible.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:42.800
And while Tej and Ramsey laugh at Roman's
outlandish theory, by the end of "F9," we're

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.170
kind of wondering if he might be onto something.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:48.810
After all, Letty and Han survived crashes
that everyone assumed killed them.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:52.910
Roman and Tej manage to survive a trip to
outer space in a car that exploded earlier

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:53.910
in the movie.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:58.320
Not to mention countless close-calls for every
member of the crew throughout the franchise.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.990
Of course, there is one person who throws
a wrench in this theory: Gisele, who died

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:04.810
at the end of "Fast &amp; Furious 6" saving Han.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:08.490
If one member of their crew died, it stands
to reason that all of them could under the

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:12.310
right circumstances — unless, of course,
Gisele is somehow still alive too.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:16.779
While we're not holding our breath, after
getting #JusticeForHan, we won't rule anything

00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:17.779
out.

00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:21.720
And if Gisele ever does return to the family,
there really may be something to Roman's bizarre

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.100
theory after all.

